  "_note": "How drug labels actually name a class, mapped from its formal EPC string. Labels are written by clinicians for clinicians and use the abbreviation: lisinopril's interaction section says 'NSAID' three times and 'nonsteroidal' not once, so matching only the EPC phrase 'Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drug' missed lisinopril + ibuprofen entirely. Every entry here was added to close a measured miss, not from imagination. Terms are matched case-insensitively at word boundaries with an optional trailing 's', so the singular covers the plural; do not add both. Keep them long enough to be unambiguous: 'PPI' would be a false positive generator, 'proton pump inhibitor' is not.",

  "Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drug [EPC]": [
